Dear Brandon, thank you for your answer, and please excuse me for the late response, but the time zone here in Greece is different. So, i will create a "fake" expression set, like an affymetrix dataset i have in R, in order to post more appropriately my questions :

> set.seed(81)

> mat <- matrix(data=sample(1:100), nrow=12136, ncol=60)

pdat <- data.frame(Condition=rep(c("Normal", "Diseased"),times=30))

new.set <- new("ExpressionSet", exprs=mat)
 pData(new.set) <-pdat

> head(pData(new.set))
  Condition
1    Normal
2  Diseased
3    Normal
4  Diseased
5    Normal
6  Diseased

> dim(new.set)
Features  Samples
   12136       60

Thus, lets suppose that i want to produce some exploratory plots for this "aritficial" microarray dataset--in detail, histogram and boxplot plots. Now, i understand very naively, that in order to use the ggplot() function, i have to use somehow:

for istance, ggplot(data, aes(x=, y=) + geom_histogram()----where data is a data frame, and x represents the log2 intensities(assume we have these above) and y the sample names. But the main problem, is when i try to create a data frame to include these two columns. the samples are 60, whereas the features-genes are 12.136 !! So, my main problem is how to move or which transformations do i have to perform in order to apply the same logic, to create the histogram above or a boxplot  ??

Best,

Efstathios

thank you for your answers

On Tuesday, January 5, 2016 at 4:34:11 AM UTC+2, Brandon Hurr wrote:
> Efstathios,
>
> It's been a while, but I have done some exploratory visualizations of microarray and RNAseq data using ggplot2. To answer your questions:
>
> 1) to my knowledge such a thing does not exist, but there might be some things out there in personal scripts and gists.
> 2) It sounds like you just need to transpose your array. If you have a matrix as you describe just wrap it in as.data.frame(t("matrix goes here")). My understanding is that you'll want some sort of normalization of your matrix before plotting.
>
> If you have more specific questions related to plotting, could you dput() a subset of your dataset and provide a better description of what you want?
>
> B
>
